course: dictionary
lesson: 12570
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The clues in the sentence show you which meaning the writer means. «Mum chopped fresh dill and stirred it into the potato salad.» The words chopped, potato salad and stirred tell you dill is a herb for cooking.
explain: Now look at this one: «Ben dropped his pencil and said, "Oops, I'm such a dill!"» Nobody is talking about food here. Calling someone a dill is a silly, friendly way of saying they have been foolish. Ask yourself which meaning fits the words around it.
explain: Use the check you already know: swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. «Oops, I'm such a herb!» sounds wrong, so the clues point to the foolish meaning.
fact: Dill is a herb with soft, feathery leaves and a fresh taste.
fact: Cooks use dill with fish, potatoes and pickles.
fact: In Australian and New Zealand slang, «a dill» means a silly or foolish person.
fact: Calling someone «a dill» is a mild, friendly insult, like calling them a twit.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, so always check the clues in the sentence.
exercise: «We put fresh dill on the salmon before we baked it.» What is dill in this sentence? | *A herb used in cooking | A sort of cloud | A small fish | A silly person
exercise: Aussie Sam laughs, «You dill, you left the gate open!» What does dill mean here? | A kind of bird | A very tall tree | *A silly or foolish person, a friendly insult | A green herb for pickles
exercise: Which words in «Gran grew dill in her garden for her pickles» tell you dill is a plant? | Gran, her | *grew, garden | pickles, Gran | in, for
test: «Mum added dill to the potatoes, and they tasted fresh and green.» What taste does dill give? | *A fresh taste | A salty, dusty taste | A sweet, sticky taste | A sour, burnt taste
test: Ella laughs, «Don't be such a dill!» What kind of word is dill here? | The name of a fish | *A mild, friendly insult | A compliment about her garden | A cooking instruction
exam: Which meaning fits best in «Tom is a dill for leaving his bag on the bus»? | *A silly or foolish person | A jar of pickles | A green herb with feathery leaves | A type of fish
